\subsection{Verification of Necessary Conditions for Reconstruction}

As previously mentioned exact image reconstruction is only possible under the assumption that the gradients provide information for there to be $d$ independent constraints on the input image, where $d$ is the number of unknowns in the input image. A weak and cheap lower bound is checking whether the number of gradients is above the number of unknowns. This was done by first calculating the number of unknowns from the input image by simply multiplying the number of input channels $C$ with the width and height of the image, leading to 3072 unknowns for images from CIFAR10 and 100 and 784 for images from MNIST. This lower bound was always met when masking was applied on even the smallest ResNet models, for example ResNet18 the smallest ResNet available, has over 11 million gradients, which would mean we would have to mask 99.97\% of the gradients in order to be below the threshold.

However, a more realistic bound as described earlier, relies on the Jacobian matrix of the gradients and unknowns having full rank. This is in practice hard calculate when dealing with networks containing millions of gradients. As the Jacobian matrix for a model using a ResNet18 architecture and CIFAR100 images would result in a matrix with approximately 11 million rows and 3072 columns, meaning there would be around 34.5 billion entries in the matrix. Attempting to compute the Jacobian using PyTorch’s \texttt{autograd} function or similar methods and then the rank would require over 200 Gb of memory.

As a solution a subset of the gradients were used to compute the Jacobian and its rank. As if full rank is achieved for a subset of gradients it also holds for the entire set, since every direction spanned by the subset is also spanned by the full matrix. Consequently the numerical rank of the Jacobian matrix was calculated for 3 images over several different gradient subset sizes and averaged for each gradient subset to form figure \ref{fig:jac_rank}. Figure \ref{fig:jac_rank_top} uses absolute magnitude to choose the subset of gradients, while figure \ref{fig:jac_rank_rand} uses random sampling.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\begin{minipage}{0.5\textwidth}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{Pictures/jac_rank_resnet18_cifar100_idlg_topk_abs_ns3_20260325_002859.png}
  \subcaption{Top gradients by size}
  \label{fig:jac_rank_top}
\end{minipage}%
\begin{minipage}{0.5\textwidth}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{Pictures/jac_rank_resnet18_cifar100_idlg_random_ns3_20260324_231841.png}
  \subcaption{Random gradients}
  \label{fig:jac_rank_rand}
\end{minipage}
\caption{Jacobian rank}
\label{fig:jac_rank}
\end{figure}

The Jacobian is calculated using PyTorch’s \texttt{autograd.grad} function to compute the first order partial derivatives with respect to the unknown for each gradient in the gradient subset. The rank of the Jacobian is computed using PyTorch’s \texttt{linalg.matrix\_rank} function, which computes the numerical rank of the matrix rather than the exact rank. It works by performing singular value decomposition (SVD) on the matrix and the number of singular values above a certain threshold is the rank of the matrix. The magnitude of a singular value explain how much the matrix changes in that direction. In theory all non-zero singular values should count towards the rank, however due to numerical noise from roundoff errors, singular values that theoretically should be zero instead appear as singular values with very small values. Therefore, counting all non zero singular values could lead to an overestimation of the matrix’s rank.

In the case of \texttt{torch.linalg.matrix\_rank(J)} the default tolerance is set by the formula: $\max(\text{atol}, \sigma_{\max}\cdot \text{rtol})$
where \texttt{atol} is the absolute tolerance which is 0 if not specified, $\sigma_{\max}$ the largest singular value, and \texttt{rtol} the relative tolerance with default value: $\text{rtol} = \max(m, n)\cdot \epsilon$
where $\max(m, n)$ is the number of rows ($m$) or columns ($n$) which ever is largest, and the $\epsilon$ is the machine precision for the dtype of the matrix.

Initially the dtype was set to the default \texttt{torch.float32} which has a machine precision value of $1.19\times 10^{-7}$. However, since numerical error increases with matrix size when calculating singular values, as more floating point operations are performed, leading to more opportunities for round off errors to accumulate, resulting in numerical noise. As a result of the machine uncertainty the rank of the Jacobian was underestimated as evident from figure \ref{fig:jac_rank}. Where the rank of the Jacobian initially grows with number of gradients reaching a rank of 3064 with the top 20,000 gradients, but then as the number of gradients increases past 20,000 all the way up to 200,000 gradients the rank falls. While this is not theoretically possible when calculating the exact rank of a matrix in linear algebra as the addition of row entries to a matrix can never decrease its rank or number of singular values. However, since the relative tolerance increases to account for the increase in numerical noise due to the increased matrix size it means several of the smaller singular values fall below the threshold and are classified as noise leading to the decrease in rank.

To account for this the dtype was changed from the standard \texttt{torch.float32} to \texttt{torch.float64} meaning more decimals were included increasing the machine precision to approximately $2.22\times 10^{-16}$ drastically bringing down the relative tolerance. By recomputing the Jacobian using dtype \texttt{float64} full rank was achieved as seen in figure (ref figure), where the numerical rank was computed independently for 100 images at different gradient subset sizes and averaged. Figure \ref{fig:jac_rank_top_64} uses absolute magnitude to choose the subset of gradients, while figure \ref{fig:jac_rank_rand_64} uses random sampling.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\begin{minipage}{0.5\textwidth}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{Pictures/jac_rank_resnet18_cifar100_idlg_topk_abs_ns10_20260408_070134.png}
  \subcaption{Top gradients by size}
  \label{fig:jac_rank_top_64}
\end{minipage}%
\begin{minipage}{0.5\textwidth}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{Pictures/jac_rank_resnet18_cifar100_idlg_random_ns10_20260408_052109.png}
  \subcaption{Random gradients}
  \label{fig:jac_rank_rand_64}
\end{minipage}
\caption{Jacobian rank}
\label{fig:jac_rank_64}
\end{figure}

The figure enforces our theory of requiring more gradients than minimal viable conditions, as achieving full rank more gradients than the number of unknowns as some gradients are redundant and linearly dependent. Resulting in, insignificant singular values and no increase in the rank. Furthermore, the number of gradients required to achieve full rank is highly dependent on the model architecture, number of model parameters, dataset and masking strategies used. As evident in the figures (make figures) below where the rank has been computed and plotted for different architectures, models and datasets. However, one thing is persistent across the plots, the number of gradients required is always (say how much) above the minimal threshold (and maybe not even close to it?). Therefore, during the experiments before attempting to reconstruct the input image, the Jacobian and its rank was computed as a safe guard to ensure reconstruction was possible.

\subsection{Implementation}

\subsubsection{Restarts}
To improve robustness and minimize the effects of bad initializations, multiple restarts were used during the optimization. Since gradient inversion is a highly non-convex optimization problem with multiple local minimas and saddle points, reconstruction quality depends on the initialization of the dummy images, where bad initialization can lead to poor reconstruction as it plateus on a saddle point or gets stuck in a local minima. To mitigate this, optimization was repeated several times from different random starting points and the reconstruction with the lowest final loss was kept. Multiple restarts improves stability and reduces the risk of drawing conclusions based on unsuccessful initializations. 

\subsubsection{L-BFGS}
To implement the L-BFGS optimizer, PyTorch's LBFGS function, It has   

In theory, L-BFGS is well-suited for smooth, low-dimensional optimization problems, as it can converge in far fewer iterations than first-order methods by exploiting curvature information. This made it an appealing candidate for the gradient-matching objective, where the number of unknowns is relatively small (3072 for CIFAR images) compared to the number of gradient observations.

\subsubsection{AdamW with L-BFGS Refinement}

A hybrid two-phase optimization strategy was implemented that combines the complementary strengths of AdamW and L-BFGS. In the first phase, AdamW with weight decay $\lambda = 10^{-5}$ and a step-decay learning rate schedule is used to explore the loss landscape. AdamW's adaptive per-parameter learning rates and momentum allow it to make rapid progress from a random initialization and navigate the highly non-convex gradient-matching objective, where L-BFGS would be prone to divergence or premature convergence to poor local minima.

Once AdamW plateaus, defined as the relative improvement in loss falling below a threshold $\epsilon_{\text{rel}}$ for \texttt{switch\_patience} consecutive iterations, the optimizer is switched to L-BFGS. At the point of switching, the dummy image tensor retains its current state, providing L-BFGS with a warm start in a region of the loss landscape already refined by AdamW. L-BFGS then exploits second-order curvature information via a limited-memory approximation of the inverse Hessian to converge precisely to a nearby local minimum that first-order methods typically cannot reach with the same accuracy.

Formally, the switch occurs at iteration $t^*$ defined as:
\begin{equation}
    t^* = \min \left\{ t \geq t_{\text{warmup}} : \sum_{s=t-p+1}^{t} \mathbf{1}\left[\frac{\ell^*_s - \ell_s}{\max(|\ell^*_s|, \epsilon)} \leq \epsilon_{\text{rel}}\right] = p \right\}
\end{equation}
where $p$ is \texttt{switch\_patience}, $\ell^*_s = \min_{j \leq s} \ell_j$ is the running best loss up to iteration $s$, and $\epsilon = 10^{-12}$ is a small constant for numerical stability. After the switch, the plateau counter and best loss tracking are reset so that the L-BFGS phase receives its own full patience budget before early stopping is triggered.

This staged approach was motivated by the observation that pure L-BFGS reconstruction is sensitive to initialization: starting from a random dummy image places the iterate far from any smooth region, causing the Hessian approximation to be unreliable in early iterations. Pure AdamW, conversely, can make steady progress but stalls in a neighbourhood of a local minimum without the ability to exploit curvature for fine-grained refinement. The hybrid strategy therefore addresses both failure modes: AdamW handles the coarse global search, while L-BFGS handles the final high-precision convergence.
